---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import blogStudyUk from '../assets/blog/study-uk.jpg';
const title = "Studying in the UK: A Practical Guide for Bangladeshi Students | CloudBridge";
const description = "UK tuition costs, entry requirements, the Student visa process, and scholarships — a complete guide to studying in the UK from CloudBridge.";
---
<Layout title={title} description={description}>

<section class="py-10 bg-white border-b border-black/5">
  <div class="max-w-container mx-auto px-4 md:px-8">
    <p class="crumb !text-ink/40"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/blogs">Blogs</a> / Study in the UK</p>
  </div>
</section>
<article class="py-10 md:py-14">
  <div class="max-w-container mx-auto px-4 md:px-8 max-w-3xl">
    <p class="course-badge">Study Destinations</p>
    <h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-bridge-950 leading-tight">Studying in the UK: A Practical Guide for Bangladeshi Students</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink/50">Updated July 2026 · 8 min read</p>
    <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
      <Image src={blogStudyUk} alt="Big Ben and the Houses of Parliament in London, a popular study destination for international students" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" widths={[480, 768, 1200]} sizes="(min-width: 768px) 700px, 100vw" />
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 text-lg text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Every year, thousands of students from Bangladesh pack a suitcase and head to the UK for a degree — and it's easy to see why. Between the shorter course lengths, the sheer range of universities, and a post-study work visa that actually lets you stay and work afterwards, the UK keeps landing near the top of the list for students weighing up where to study abroad. If you're at the "is this actually realistic for me" stage, here's what studying in the UK really looks like, cost by cost and step by step.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">Why so many students choose the UK</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">A few things come up again and again when we talk to students who've made the move. Degrees are shorter than in a lot of other countries — a bachelor's is typically three years and a master's just one — which means less tuition overall and a faster route into the job market. The UK is also home to a huge range of globally recognised universities, so there's usually a realistic option whether your goal is a Russell Group name for your CV or a smaller university with lower fees and a tighter-knit campus.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Then there's the Graduate Route visa, which currently lets international graduates stay and work in the UK for two years after finishing an undergraduate or master's degree (three years after a PhD) without needing a job offer first. For a lot of students, that window to gain UK work experience is as big a draw as the degree itself.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">What it actually costs</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Tuition fees for international students vary a lot depending on the course and university — as a rough guide, expect somewhere between £10,000 and £20,000 a year for most undergraduate arts, business, and social science courses, rising to £20,000–£38,000+ for lab-based subjects like engineering, computer science, or medicine. Postgraduate fees follow a similar pattern, often on the lower end since the course only runs a year.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">For your visa application, the Home Office also expects you to show proof of funds for living costs — roughly £1,023 a month if you're studying in London, or around £820 a month outside London, for up to nine months. In practice, day-to-day living (rent, food, transport, phone) tends to sit somewhere in that range depending on the city, with London obviously at the pricier end and cities like Sheffield, Coventry, or Glasgow noticeably cheaper.</p>
    <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl bg-bridge-50 p-5 border border-bridge-100">
      <p class="text-sm text-bridge-900"><strong>Quick tip:</strong> Choosing a university outside London can often cut your total budget by 25–35% without any real difference in degree recognition — something we walk through with every student during shortlisting.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">Entry requirements and the application process</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Most undergraduate applications go through UCAS, while postgraduate applications are usually made directly to the university. You'll generally need your academic transcripts (SSC and HSC, or equivalent), an English proficiency score — IELTS is the most widely accepted, though some universities accept alternatives or an MOI (Medium of Instruction) certificate — a statement of purpose, and one or two academic references.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Once you receive and accept an offer, the university issues a CAS (Confirmation of Acceptance for Studies) letter, which is the document you'll use to apply for your Student visa. From there it's financial evidence, a visa application, biometrics, and — for most Bangladeshi applicants — a straightforward online process rather than an in-person interview.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">Scholarships and funding</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">A good number of UK universities offer merit-based scholarships and international student bursaries that can meaningfully reduce your tuition — often applied automatically once you're admitted, though some do require a separate application. It's worth checking early, since several close before the general admission deadline. If you'd rather not comb through every university's funding page yourself, this is exactly the kind of thing we check for you as part of a free consultation.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 border border-black/10 rounded-2xl divide-y divide-black/10 bg-white">
      <div>
        <button class="faq-btn w-full text-left px-6 py-4 font-display font-semibold text-bridge-900 flex justify-between items-center">Do I need IELTS to study in the UK?<span class="faq-icon transition-transform">+</span></button>
        <p class="faq-answer px-6 pb-5 text-sm text-ink/70">Most universities ask for IELTS, but many also accept PTE Academic, Duolingo English Test, or an MOI certificate depending on your previous education. Your counsellor can confirm exactly what your target university accepts.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <button class="faq-btn w-full text-left px-6 py-4 font-display font-semibold text-bridge-900 flex justify-between items-center">Can I work part-time while studying?<span class="faq-icon transition-transform">+</span></button>
        <p class="faq-answer hidden px-6 pb-5 text-sm text-ink/70">Yes — most Student visa holders can work up to 20 hours a week during term time and full-time during scheduled holidays, which many students use to cover part of their living costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <button class="faq-btn w-full text-left px-6 py-4 font-display font-semibold text-bridge-900 flex justify-between items-center">How long does the UK student visa take?<span class="faq-icon transition-transform">+</span></button>
        <p class="faq-answer hidden px-6 pb-5 text-sm text-ink/70">Standard processing outside the UK is usually around three weeks, though we recommend applying as soon as your CAS is issued to leave a comfortable buffer before your course starts.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 rounded-2xl bg-dusk-gradient text-white p-8 text-center">
      <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Thinking about the UK for your degree?</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-white/75 text-sm max-w-md mx-auto">Talk to a CloudBridge counsellor about universities, costs, and your visa timeline — free, no obligation.</p>
      <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
        <a href="/#lead-form" class="btn-primary nav-link inline-flex">Get Free Consultation</a>
        <a href="/dest-uk" class="btn-secondary nav-link inline-flex">Explore UK Universities</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8"><a href="/blogs" class="nav-link text-sm font-semibold text-bridge-800 hover:underline">&larr; Back to all articles</a></p>
  </div>
</article>

</Layout>
